---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government ERP — Odoo for Thai Public Sector | Ecosoft"
  description="Ecosoft's government ERP modules are purpose-built for Thai public sector compliance — budget encumbrance, government procurement, multi-fund accounting, and audit-ready reporting."
>


  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="erpnext-hero-inner">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Government ERP</p>
          <h1>The only open-source ERP built for Thai government compliance. Running in production since 2018.</h1>
          <p class="hero-lead">
            Thai government agencies have requirements that commercial ERP systems handle poorly and open-source systems
            ignore entirely. Ecosoft has spent years building the modules that fill this gap — budget encumbrance,
            government procurement workflows, multi-fund accounting, and audit-ready reporting — all running on Odoo.
          </p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Talk to a Government ERP Specialist</a>
            <a href="/case-studies/nstda" class="btn btn-outline">See NSTDA Case Study</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="info-card" style="padding:2rem;">
            <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Reference installation</h3>
            <p><strong>NSTDA</strong> — National Science and Technology Development Agency</p>
            <p>Replaced SAP in 2018. 7+ years of continuous operation. 500+ staff across multiple research centres.</p>
            <a href="/case-studies/nstda" class="btn btn-outline">Read the case study →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE CHALLENGE -->
  <section class="section" style="background: var(--surface);">
    <div class="container">
      <div style="max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; text-align:center;">
        <h2>Why commercial ERP fails government agencies</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Government agencies operate under procurement regulations, budget control structures, and reporting
          requirements that generic ERP systems never address out of the box. The result: expensive consultants,
          fragile workarounds, and compliance risk.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap:1.5rem; margin-top:2.5rem;">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Budget encumbrance is manual</h4>
          <p>Commercial ERPs treat budget as advisory. Government requires hard stops when encumbrance limits are reached — before commitments are made, not after.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Procurement compliance gaps</h4>
          <p>Thai government procurement regulations mandate specific approval workflows, quotation thresholds, and documentation requirements that standard purchase modules skip.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Multi-fund accounting complexity</h4>
          <p>Research agencies and government departments track income and expenditure by programme, project, and fund — not just by department. Standard chart-of-accounts structures collapse under this.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>SAP/Oracle licensing burden</h4>
          <p>Public budgets shouldn't be consumed by vendor licence fees. Every baht spent on SAP licensing is a baht not spent on the agency's mission.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MODULES -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 style="text-align:center;">What we implement</h2>
      <p class="section-lead" style="text-align:center;">Purpose-built modules on top of Odoo CE, all released as open-source under OCA-compatible licences.</p>
      <div class="cards-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap:1.5rem; margin-top:2.5rem;">

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">📋</div>
          <h4>Budget Planning & Encumbrance Control</h4>
          <p>Annual budget allocation by department, programme, and project. Real-time encumbrance tracking. Hard budget controls preventing over-commitment at point of purchase order creation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">🏛️</div>
          <h4>Government Procurement</h4>
          <p>Compliant purchase workflows aligned with Thai government procurement regulations — quotation thresholds, committee approvals, contract registration, and supplier evaluation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">💰</div>
          <h4>Multi-fund Accounting</h4>
          <p>Income and expenditure tracked by project, programme, and funding source simultaneously. Financial reports sliced by any combination of dimensions — without custom development per report.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">📑</div>
          <h4>Contract Management</h4>
          <p>Contract register with milestone tracking, payment schedules, retention management, and deadline alerts. Full audit trail from RFQ to final payment.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">🏗️</div>
          <h4>Asset Management</h4>
          <p>Government-standard asset register, depreciation schedules aligned with public-sector accounting standards, disposal and transfer workflows, and physical asset verification.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">👥</div>
          <h4>HR & Payroll</h4>
          <p>Employee records, leave management, and Thai government payroll compliance — including civil servant allowances, welfare deductions, and government pension contributions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">📊</div>
          <h4>Financial Reporting</h4>
          <p>Government-standard financial statements, budget execution reports, and management dashboards. Reports that meet both internal governance and external audit requirements.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <div class="info-card-icon">🔐</div>
          <h4>Role-based Access & Audit</h4>
          <p>Granular permission structures aligned with segregation-of-duties requirements. Complete audit log of every transaction, approval, and system access event.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY OPEN SOURCE FOR GOVERNMENT -->
  <section class="section" style="background: var(--surface);">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="cta-strip-grid" style="align-items:start;">
        <div>
          <h2>Why open-source is the right choice for government</h2>
          <p>Government agencies have a duty to the public that commercial software vendors don't share. Open-source ERP delivers on that duty in ways proprietary systems cannot.</p>
          <ul style="margin-top:1.5rem; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.75rem; list-style:none; padding:0;">
            <li>✓ <strong>No annual licence fees</strong> — budget is spent on mission, not vendor rent</li>
            <li>✓ <strong>Full source code access</strong> — no dependency on a single vendor's survival</li>
            <li>✓ <strong>Technology sovereignty</strong> — any qualified developer can maintain the system</li>
            <li>✓ <strong>Transparent audit trail</strong> — no black-box logic in financial calculations</li>
            <li>✓ <strong>Long-term maintainability</strong> — Odoo has been in production since 2005</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card" style="padding:2rem;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">NSTDA saved substantially vs SAP</h3>
          <p>NSTDA's annual licence cost savings compared to their previous SAP installation have been reinvested into system improvements and additional module development.</p>
          <p>The system now covers multiple departments and research centres — with the same annual operating cost as a fraction of the previous SAP licence alone.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/nstda" class="btn btn-outline">Read the NSTDA case study →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA STRIP -->
  <section class="cta-strip">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Running a Thai government agency or public institution?</h2>
      <p>Talk to our government ERP team — we'll walk you through our reference installation at NSTDA and assess fit for your organisation.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Request a Government ERP Consultation</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
